{"animath":"0.1","canvas":{"height":540,"width":960},"kind":"concept","level":"secondary","scenes":[{"caption":"Velocity is speed with a direction","duration_ms":9000,"id":"s1","narration":"An athlete sprints 100 metres in 20 seconds. Her speed is distance over time: 100 divided by 20, which is 5 metres per second. Velocity adds one thing — direction. She ran to the right, so her velocity is 5 metres per second to the right.","steps":[{"at_ms":0,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"title","kind":"text","size":34,"text":"Speed or velocity?","x":80,"y":70}},{"at_ms":400,"do":"show","shape":{"color":"#2471a3","id":"formula","kind":"latex","size":40,"tex":"v = \\frac{d}{t}","x":80,"y":170}},{"at_ms":1000,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"track","kind":"line","stroke":"#5d6d7e","width":4,"x1":120,"x2":840,"y1":320,"y2":320}},{"at_ms":1300,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"run0","kind":"text","size":24,"text":"0 m","x":100,"y":370}},{"at_ms":1500,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"run100","kind":"text","size":24,"text":"100 m","x":820,"y":370}},{"at_ms":1700,"do":"show","shape":{"cx":120,"cy":320,"fill":"#f5cba7","id":"athlete","kind":"circle","r":20,"stroke":"#8e5a2b","width":2}},{"at_ms":2200,"do":"move","dur_ms":2500,"target":"athlete","to":{"cx":840,"cy":320}},{"at_ms":5000,"do":"show","shape":{"color":"#1f7a3a","id":"work","kind":"latex","size":36,"tex":"v = \\frac{100\\ \\text{m}}{20\\ \\text{s}} = 5\\ \\text{m/s}","x":80,"y":450}},{"at_ms":6200,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"dir","kind":"text","size":24,"text":"direction: to the right  →  this is velocity","x":80,"y":510}},{"at_ms":7400,"do":"caption","text":"Velocity is speed with a direction attached."}]},{"caption":"Distance–time graphs: the gradient is the speed","duration_ms":10000,"id":"s2","narration":"On a distance–time graph, time runs along the bottom and distance runs up the side. This line climbs 120 metres in 60 seconds. The gradient is rise over run: 120 divided by 60, which is 2 metres per second. Steeper line, faster motion.","steps":[{"at_ms":0,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"title","kind":"text","size":34,"text":"Reading a distance–time graph","x":80,"y":70}},{"at_ms":400,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"xAxis","kind":"line","stroke":"#2c3e50","width":3,"x1":120,"x2":760,"y1":420,"y2":420}},{"at_ms":700,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"yAxis","kind":"line","stroke":"#2c3e50","width":3,"x1":120,"x2":120,"y1":420,"y2":100}},{"at_ms":1000,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"xL","kind":"text","size":22,"text":"time: 0 → 60 s","x":560,"y":460}},{"at_ms":1200,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"yL","kind":"text","size":22,"text":"distance: 0 → 120 m","x":140,"y":110}},{"at_ms":1600,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"gline","kind":"line","stroke":"#c0392b","width":5,"x1":120,"x2":720,"y1":420,"y2":120}},{"at_ms":2600,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"rise","kind":"text","size":24,"text":"rise = 120 m","x":740,"y":270}},{"at_ms":3100,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"run","kind":"text","size":24,"text":"run = 60 s","x":400,"y":470}},{"at_ms":3600,"do":"show","shape":{"color":"#1f7a3a","id":"work","kind":"latex","size":36,"tex":"\\text{speed} = \\frac{\\text{rise}}{\\text{run}} = \\frac{120\\ \\text{m}}{60\\ \\text{s}} = 2\\ \\text{m/s}","x":80,"y":510}},{"at_ms":5200,"do":"emphasize","target":"gline"},{"at_ms":6000,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"steep","kind":"text","size":24,"text":"Steeper line = faster. Flat line = stopped.","x":480,"y":70}},{"at_ms":7200,"do":"caption","text":"Distance–time gradient = speed. Rise over run."}]},{"caption":"Velocity–time graphs: slope is acceleration, area is distance","duration_ms":11000,"id":"s3","narration":"Now the vertical axis is velocity. The line rises from 0 to 20 metres per second in 10 seconds. Its slope is 20 divided by 10 — an acceleration of 2 metres per second squared. And the area under the line is the distance: half times 10 times 20, which is 100 metres.","steps":[{"at_ms":0,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"title","kind":"text","size":34,"text":"Reading a velocity–time graph","x":80,"y":70}},{"at_ms":400,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"xAxis","kind":"line","stroke":"#2c3e50","width":3,"x1":120,"x2":760,"y1":420,"y2":420}},{"at_ms":700,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"yAxis","kind":"line","stroke":"#2c3e50","width":3,"x1":120,"x2":120,"y1":420,"y2":100}},{"at_ms":1000,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"xL","kind":"text","size":22,"text":"time: 0 → 10 s","x":560,"y":460}},{"at_ms":1200,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"yL","kind":"text","size":22,"text":"velocity: 0 → 20 m/s","x":140,"y":110}},{"at_ms":1600,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"gline","kind":"line","stroke":"#7d3c98","width":5,"x1":120,"x2":720,"y1":420,"y2":120}},{"at_ms":2600,"do":"show","shape":{"color":"#1f7a3a","id":"slope","kind":"latex","size":34,"tex":"a = \\frac{20\\ \\text{m/s}}{10\\ \\text{s}} = 2\\ \\text{m/s}^2","x":80,"y":505}},{"at_ms":3800,"do":"show","shape":{"fill":"#d6eaf8","id":"area","kind":"polygon","points":[[120,420],[720,420],[720,120]],"stroke":"#2471a3","width":2}},{"at_ms":4400,"do":"show","shape":{"color":"#1f7a3a","id":"dist","kind":"latex","size":32,"tex":"d = \\text{area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 10 \\times 20 = 100\\ \\text{m}","x":440,"y":240}},{"at_ms":5800,"do":"emphasize","target":"area"},{"at_ms":6600,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"note","kind":"text","size":22,"text":"Triangle, not rectangle — velocity is changing.","x":440,"y":290}},{"at_ms":8200,"do":"caption","text":"Slope = acceleration. Area under the line = distance."}]},{"caption":"Check the thinking","duration_ms":9000,"id":"s4","narration":"Here is the classic trap. A runner jogs 400 metres down a straight track, turns, and jogs the 400 metres back. That takes 100 seconds in total. The distance covered is 800 metres, so the speed is 8 metres per second. But she finished exactly where she started: her displacement is zero, so her velocity is zero metres per second.","steps":[{"at_ms":0,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"title","kind":"text","size":34,"text":"Check the thinking","x":80,"y":70}},{"at_ms":400,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"myth","kind":"text","size":28,"text":"❌ “Speed and velocity are the same thing.”","x":80,"y":170}},{"at_ms":1200,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"track","kind":"line","stroke":"#5d6d7e","width":4,"x1":120,"x2":840,"y1":300,"y2":300}},{"at_ms":1500,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"out","kind":"arrow","stroke":"#c0392b","width":5,"x1":220,"x2":740,"y1":250,"y2":250}},{"at_ms":1800,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"outL","kind":"text","size":24,"text":"out: 400 m","x":430,"y":240}},{"at_ms":2200,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"back","kind":"arrow","stroke":"#2471a3","width":5,"x1":740,"x2":220,"y1":350,"y2":350}},{"at_ms":2500,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"backL","kind":"text","size":24,"text":"back: 400 m","x":430,"y":390}},{"at_ms":3100,"do":"show","shape":{"color":"#1f7a3a","id":"work","kind":"latex","size":34,"tex":"\\text{speed} = \\frac{800\\ \\text{m}}{100\\ \\text{s}} = 8\\ \\text{m/s},\\quad v = \\frac{0\\ \\text{m}}{100\\ \\text{s}} = 0","x":80,"y":450}},{"at_ms":4800,"do":"show","shape":{"id":"fix","kind":"text","size":24,"text":"Velocity counts displacement — and she ended where she began.","x":80,"y":510}},{"at_ms":6200,"do":"caption","text":"Speed uses distance. Velocity uses displacement."}]}],"subject":"science","title":"Motion: speed, velocity and graphs","topic":"phys-kinematics"}